### How do you safely remove elements during iteration?

The most common mistake in Java collections is modifying a list inside a for-each loop.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class SafeRemovalDemo {
    record Account(String id, String status, BigDecimal balance) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Account> accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", "ACTIVE", new BigDecimal("150000")),
            new Account("CLOSED-001", "CLOSED", BigDecimal.ZERO),
            new Account("CUR3001", "ACTIVE", new BigDecimal("75000")),
            new Account("CLOSED-002", "CLOSED", BigDecimal.ZERO)
        ));

        // WRONG: for-each + remove → CME
        try {
            for (Account acc : accounts) {
                if (acc.status().equals("CLOSED")) {
                    accounts.remove(acc);
                }
            }
        } catch (ConcurrentModificationException e) {
            System.out.println("CME caught: " + e.getClass().getSimpleName());
        }

        // FIX 1: removeIf (Java 8) – BEST
        accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", "ACTIVE", new BigDecimal("150000")),
            new Account("CLOSED-001", "CLOSED", BigDecimal.ZERO),
            new Account("CUR3001", "ACTIVE", new BigDecimal("75000")),
            new Account("CLOSED-002", "CLOSED", BigDecimal.ZERO)
        ));
        accounts.removeIf(acc -> acc.status().equals("CLOSED"));
        System.out.println("removeIf result: " + accounts.size() + " accounts");

        // FIX 2: Iterator.remove()
        accounts = new ArrayList<>(List.of(
            new Account("SAV2001", "ACTIVE", new BigDecimal("150000")),
            new Account("CLOSED-001", "CLOSED", BigDecimal.ZERO)
        ));
        Iterator<Account> it = accounts.iterator();
        while (it.hasNext()) {
            if (it.next().status().equals("CLOSED")) {
                it.remove();
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Iterator.remove result: " + accounts.size() + " accounts");
    }
}
```

```text
CME caught: ConcurrentModificationException
removeIf result: 2 accounts
Iterator.remove result: 1 accounts
```

### How does removeIf work on Maps?

Maps do not have a direct removeIf method, but `entrySet().removeIf()` works because `entrySet()` returns a view backed by the map.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class MapRemoveIfDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, BigDecimal> balances = new HashMap<>();
        balances.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("150000"));
        balances.put("DORMANT-001", BigDecimal.ZERO);
        balances.put("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("75000"));
        balances.put("DORMANT-002", BigDecimal.ZERO);

        // Remove all zero-balance accounts
        balances.entrySet().removeIf(e -> e.getValue().compareTo(BigDecimal.ZERO) == 0);
        System.out.println("After cleanup: " + balances);

        // Remove by key pattern
        balances.keySet().removeIf(key -> key.startsWith("DORMANT"));
        System.out.println("After key filter: " + balances);
    }
}
```

```text
After cleanup: {SAV2001=150000, CUR3001=75000}
After key filter: {SAV2001=150000, CUR3001=75000}
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake 1: Using remove(index) in a forward loop

```java
// WRONG: indices shift after each removal
for (int i = 0; i < list.size(); i++) {
    if (condition(list.get(i))) {
        list.remove(i); // next element shifts to index i, gets skipped!
    }
}

// FIX: iterate backward
for (int i = list.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
    if (condition(list.get(i))) {
        list.remove(i); // elements after i don't matter (already processed)
    }
}

// BEST: use removeIf
list.removeIf(e -> condition(e));
```

#### Mistake 2: Assuming CME means multi-threading

```java
// CME happens in SINGLE-threaded code too!
// It's about modifying during iteration, not about threads.
for (String s : list) {
    list.add("new"); // CME! Single thread.
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **removeIf is optimized for ArrayList.** ArrayList's removeIf implementation uses a bitset to mark elements for removal, then shifts remaining elements in one pass. This is more efficient than calling iterator.remove() n times (which shifts elements on each removal).

- **removeIf returns boolean.** It returns true if any elements were removed, false otherwise. Useful for logging: `if (accounts.removeIf(condition)) log.info("Cleaned up accounts")`.

- **ConcurrentHashMap.entrySet().removeIf().** This is thread-safe. Multiple threads can call removeIf concurrently without external synchronization.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "Why does for-each + remove throw CME?" – for-each uses an iterator; list.remove changes modCount without updating expectedModCount.
- "What is the cleanest way to remove elements?" – removeIf(predicate).
- "How do you remove from a Map during iteration?" – map.entrySet().removeIf().
- "Is removeIf thread-safe?" – On ConcurrentHashMap, yes. On ArrayList, no.
